    "information": "*From the Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (AAA) page*  \n- **Age** – Abdominal aortic aneurysms occur most often in people age 65 and older.  \n- **Tobacco use (smoking)** – Smoking is the strongest risk factor for aortic aneurysms.  \n- **Family history** – Having a family history of abdominal aortic aneurysms increases the risk.  \n\n*From the Thoracic Aortic Aneurysm (TAA) page*  \n- **Age** – Thoracic aortic aneurysms occur most often in people age 65 and older.  \n- **Smoking and tobacco use** – If you smoke or chew tobacco, it can worsen an aneurysm.  \n- **Family history** – You’re more likely to get an aortic aneurysm and rupture if you have a parent, brother, sister or child with one.  \n\n*Brain aneurysm risk factors* – No brain aneurysm information was found in the pages provided, so common risk factors shared by all three aneurysms (abdominal aortic, brain, thoracic aortic) cannot be fully determined from these sources.\n\n**",
